Clockwork syntax highlighting for Vim and Neovim
================================================

Syntax support for the Clockwork / LPC language used by latproc controllers:
`*.cw` and `*.lpc` files.

Install
-------

Straight from the internet:

    curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/latproc/clockwork-syntax/master/install.sh | sh

From a checkout:

    ./install.sh

Either way the runtime files land in a Vim package directory, so nothing in
`~/.vim/syntax` is touched and no runtimepath has to be edited:

    ~/.vim/pack/clockwork/start/clockwork/

Neovim is pointed at the same package with a symlink when it has a config
directory of its own.  `--uninstall` removes both.

Layout
------

    install.sh                              the installer above
    tools/check-keywords.sh                 diff the syntax against the lexer
    vi/clockwork/syntax/clockwork.vim       the syntax file
    vi/clockwork/ftdetect/clockwork.vim     *.cw and *.lpc file type detection
    vi/clockwork/doc/clockwork.txt          :help clockwork

Keeping up with the language
----------------------------

`iod/src/cwlang.lpp` in the latproc tree is the authority for what counts as a
keyword; `cwlang.ypp` supplies the parser token names.  The syntax file is
compared against the lexer mechanically:

    tools/check-keywords.sh /path/to/iod/src/cwlang.lpp

With no argument it looks for `$LATPROC/iod/src/cwlang.lpp`, then for
`../latproc/iod/src/cwlang.lpp` beside this repository.  It exits 0 when the
two keyword sets agree, and 1 with a list of the differences when they do not.
Run it after any language change, and before committing a syntax update.
It only compares the lexer-derived groups; `cwBuiltin` and `cwConstant` come
from the run-time class registry and are checked by hand.

See `:help clockwork` for what each highlight group means.
